---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { formatUpdated, lastUpdated } from "../lib/updated";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const chapters = (await getCollection("chapters")).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const updated = formatUpdated(lastUpdated("src/pages/index.astro"));
---

<Base title="himmel: sky rendering for WebGPU" description="Small, composable TypeScript + WebGPU libraries for rendering the sky.">
    <h1>himmel</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        A family of small, independent, composable TypeScript + WebGPU libraries for rendering the sky. Each one drops
        into an existing renderer without pulling in the rest.
    </p>
    <p id="about">
        What for: a sky that is correct for a given place, date and hour, solar and lunar eclipses, stars, the Moon
        with its phase and libration, clouds, and physically based atmospheric scattering, including the refraction
        that lifts a low Sun above the horizon and the aerial perspective that fades distant geometry into haze. The
        same code precomputes what real-time rendering needs to stay fast: scattering look-up tables you can tune and
        recompute yourself, and environment maps for image-based lighting, recomputed live as the Sun moves. Where a
        computed sky is not wanted, the classic image based route stays open, with HDR sources and several
        projections. Everything is compute-first WGSL, plain functions, no scene graph.
    </p>
    <p>
        himmel succeeds{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/cgcostume/osghimmel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">osgHimmel</a>, the C++ sky
        renderer behind a 2012{" "}
        <a
            href="https://daniellimberger.de/resources/2012%20%E2%80%93%20Mueller%20%28now%20Limberger%29%20%E2%80%93%20Photorealistisches%20Rendering%20atmosphaerischer%20Effekte%20in%20geovirtuellen%203D-Umgebungen%20in%20Echtzeit.pdf"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener">master's thesis, Photorealistic Real-Time Rendering of Atmospheric Effects in Geovirtual
            3D Environments</a>, written in German, and the paper{" "}
        <a href="https://daniellimberger.de/publications.html#abstract-2012-vmv-sky-phenomena" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Single-Pass Rendering of Day and Night Sky Phenomena</a> (videos:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t8ibsEl0kAI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">computed sky</a>,{" "}
        <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y8UMaM_Wj-o" target="_blank" rel="noopener">texture mapped skies</a>).
    </p>
    <p class="status">
        <span class="beta">beta</span> Work in progress. The packages are usable but their APIs can still change.
    </p>
    <ul class="chapter-list">
        {
            chapters.map((c) => (
                <li>
                    <a href={`${base}/${c.id}/`}>
                        <span class="mono">{c.data.package}</span>
                        {c.data.planned && <span class="planned">planned</span>}
                        <p>{c.data.summary}</p>
                    </a>
                </li>
            ))
        }
    </ul>
    <p>
        Götz Hoeppe's{" "}
        <a href="https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691124537/why-the-sky-is-blue" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Why the Sky Is Blue</a> (the original is German:{" "}
        <a href="https://d-nb.info/956368794" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Blau, die Farbe des Himmels</a>, Spektrum
        Akademischer Verlag, 1999) follows two and a half thousand years of asking why the sky looks the way it does,
        from myth to atmospheric physics. It is what brought this subject close in the first place, and a good
        reminder of how much of what we understand began with a lot of people looking up and refusing to let the
        question go.
    </p>
    <p>
        The other half of that came from the work of{" "}
        <a href="http://www-evasion.imag.fr/Membres/Eric.Bruneton/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eric Bruneton</a>,
        with Fabrice Neyret, on precomputed atmospheric scattering, from the same years as the thesis. Less a basis for
        the original than the reason to take the subject apart and arrive at an own implementation of it. Both shaped
        osgHimmel back then rather than this port, and both are still worth the time.
    </p>
    <p>
        What finally started this port was Maxime Heckel's{" "}
        <a href="https://blog.maximeheckel.com/posts/on-rendering-the-sky-sunsets-and-planets/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            On Rendering the Sky, Sunsets, and Planets</a> from May 2026, which builds atmospheric scattering up from
        raymarching to look-up tables in WebGL, with a widget for every step. Excellent, and proof that all of this
        now fits in a browser.
    </p>
    <p class="page-updated">Last updated {updated}.</p>
</Base>
